@Items/inventory
The items/inventory editor menu allows editing two things, which are the
item definitions and the inventory lists.

:dl;Item definition list
On this screen, you can select an item definition to edit. You can also add
a new item definition. Item definition 0 cannot be edited, since 0 means the
lack of an item.

:de;Item definition editor
You can edit the following parameters:

þ Name: The visible name of the item. This name can be used for display
during the game and can also be used to refer to this item in scripts. The
name cannot be empty.

þ Class: The class number of the item, from 0 to 127. You can define your
own item classes and can use them to group items by classes.

þ Description: A description of the item, which is only used for display,
if you have defined a part of the item window to display descriptions or
if descriptions are displayed separately. The description is not required.

þ Element: The element number, which is not currently meaningful, but you
can use it for your own uses.

þ Weight: The weight of this item. The total weight of all items in an
inventory (when each weight is multiplied by the corresponding quantity
of that item) cannot exceed the inventory's strength.

þ Max heap: The maximum number of this kind of item that is allowed in a
single inventory slot. The inventory may also have a max heap setting which
also limits it; the smaller limit is used. This does not prevent having
multiple heaps of the same kind of items within an inventory list.

þ Script: The script for the item. The program for the game world may load
and execute the script of an item when the item is invoked, and may have
labels (specific to the world) for various functions of the items. (The
script is not required.)

þ Flags: There are standard flags and custom flags. The custom flags have no
inherent use and you can use them for your own use. See the below section
for the working of standard flags.

:df;Item definition flags
The standard flags are as follows:

þ Not discardable: Prevents the player from discarding this item when the
item window is displayed. This should be set for a "key item".

þ Single heap: Prevents multiple heaps of this kind of item from occuring
within a single inventory list. If the max heap limit has been reached,
then no more of that item can be added to that inventory.

þ Hide quantity: Hides the quantity of the item in the item window. This
flag should be set for a unique "key item".

þ Unidentified: This flag does nothing by itself, but may be useful in
combination with your own codes in order to change the display of an item
which has not yet been identified by the player. The value of this flag
can be modified at run time, and will be saved in the save game file.

